# RunOS safety rules

RunOS tools act on live infrastructure that other people depend on. These rules
apply to every RunOS call, in every conversation.

## Confirm before any deploy or write

Ask the user before you call a tool that changes anything. Name two things in the
question: the exact resource, and the exact cluster. Then wait for a yes.

"Shall I deploy?" is not enough. "Shall I deploy app `<id>` to cluster `<id>`?" is.

A deploy is not reversible by an undo. Treat it as permanent.

## `confirm=true` records an agreement, it does not create one

A destructive RunOS tool refuses the call unless you pass `confirm: true`. That flag
means the user agreed to **this target**. It is a record of a decision they made.

Pass it only after the user said yes to that exact resource and that exact cluster.

Never pass it to get past a refusal. Never pass it because the previous call failed
and you are retrying. Never pass it on a target the user did not name. If you are
unsure whether the agreement covers this call, ask again. Asking twice costs a
sentence. Guessing costs their infrastructure.

## Warn before a sensitive read

Some RunOS tools return a credential: a password, a connection string, a private
key, a token. Their output enters the conversation and stays there.

Before you call one, tell the user what you are about to read and why you need it.
Then read the minimum. Do not fetch a whole credential set to answer a question
about one field.

Never write a credential into a file, a commit, a comment or a log line. Never
repeat one back in your summary. Refer to it by name.

## Know which server you are on

RunOS ships four MCP servers. The split between them is the primary access
control, so the server a tool sits on tells you most of what it can do:

- `runos` performs no mutation.
- `runos-sensitive-read` reads credentials, kubeconfigs and private keys.
- `runos-write` mutates.
- `runos-sensitive-write` mutates AND can expose a credential or run a command
  on a machine. This is the server that carries `deploy`, `apps_build`, `run`,
  `nodes_join-command`, `services_postgresql_exec-sql`,
  `services_postgresql_drop-database` and `storage-groups_wipe-device`. It is
  the highest risk category RunOS has, and a deploy needs it.

### The read server is not credential free

Do not treat `runos` as safe to call blind. It performs no mutation, but on a
CLI older than CLI tool manifest 45.0.0 the plain read tier still returns real
secrets for eight commands:

    services_grafana_credentials          Grafana admin username and password
    services_litellm_credentials          masterKey, uiUsername, uiPassword
    services_langfuse_credentials         initialUserPassword, project secret key
    services_vector_credentials           clickhousePassword
    services_clickhouse_credentials       admin and readonly passwords
    services_litellm_api-keys             the configured AI provider keys
    services_minio_get-object             stored object content
    services_netbird-server_credentials   adminEmail and adminPassword

All eight are `sensitive_read` from CLI tool manifest 45.0.0 onward. Until the
user's CLI is on that manifest or later, all eight are on the read server.

The NetBird one is the reason this list is written out rather than derived. Its
declared output named only two URLs, so an audit read from the manifest could
not see the password at all.

Two `credentials` commands correctly stay on `read`, because their output really
is host, port and URL only: `services_prometheus_credentials` and
`services_traefik_credentials`.

So apply the "warn before a sensitive read" rule above to those tools even
though they are on the read server. Call `manifest_update` and check the
manifest version if you need to know which side of 45.0.0 the user is on.

## Which servers are actually on

This plugin's `mcp.json` declares ONE server, `runos`. The other three are not
declared, so they cannot be enabled from this plugin. A user who wants them adds
them to their own MCP configuration, and the README says how.

That is the intent, not a guarantee about your session. Check what you can
actually see before you assume a write tool is unreachable, and tell the user
which RunOS servers are live in this conversation. If you can see
`runos-sensitive-write`, say so plainly: somebody added it deliberately, and it
carries `deploy` and `storage-groups_wipe-device`.

If a tool you need is missing, do not work around it. Tell the user which server
carries it and let them decide whether to add it.

## Do not rely on the confirmation prompt

This plugin ships a Cursor `beforeMCPExecution` hook that returns `ask` for the
three non-read servers, and for any read tool whose NAME looks credential shaped.
The hook matches on the name, not on the list above, so it also prompts for tools
that list does not name, such as `services_kafka_credentials`.
It is a backstop, not the control. It does not run everywhere:

- Cursor does not load `beforeMCPExecution` or `sessionStart` hooks in a cloud
  agent, so neither the prompt nor the session check exists there.
- A host that is not Cursor reads the skills and `mcp.json` and ignores
  `com.cursor/` entirely, so it has no hook at all.

Your own confirmation, asked in words and naming the resource and the cluster, is
the control that works in every one of those places. Ask anyway. Never treat the
absence of a prompt as the user's agreement.

## Pass absolute paths

The RunOS MCP server's working directory is the plugin folder, not the user's
project. The Agent Plugins specification requires that: when `cwd` is omitted a
client MUST use the plugin root, and `${PLUGIN_ROOT}` is the same thing, so
there is no form of `mcp.json` that points the server at the workspace.

Several RunOS tools read the filesystem. So every path argument you pass, such
as `yaml_file`, `config` or `out`, must be an ABSOLUTE path. A relative path
resolves inside the installed plugin folder, where `apps_diff` answers "no
runos*.yaml found in current directory" and `apps_pull` would write its output.

## Read before write

Run the read-only preview first: `apps_diff`, `services_diff`, a status tool. Show
the user what will change. Then ask.

## A block is not a user decision

Cursor can stop a tool call on its own, before the user sees anything. When it
does, you are told:

    Tool rejected: User rejected MCP: <tool> - User chose to skip.

That string is used for more than one thing. It covers a real click on Skip, and
it also covers an automatic block the user never saw. You cannot tell them apart,
so you must not claim either.

- Never tell the user they skipped, declined or rejected a call. Say the host
  blocked it, and that they may not have been asked.
- When the blocked call was an ordinary read (`mcp_bootstrap`, `mcp_topics_show`,
  `clusters_list` and the like), this plugin's own hook returned `allow`. The
  block did not come from RunOS.
- Cursor exposes a native approval retry for a blocked call. Use it once, for a
  read-only, non-credential tool only, so the user gets a real prompt they can
  answer. If it is blocked again, stop and tell them.
- Never use that retry to get past a block on a write or a credential read. A
  real deny and an automatic block look identical, and you must treat the pair as
  a deny.

If the RunOS tools then disappear, or a call fails during live tool discovery,
the server has died in this session. Say that, ask the user to reload the window,
and stop. Do NOT report it as a sign-in problem.
